d look like when accounting for non-visual performance. Looking at the parameter efficiency metrics, FTM has an incredibly high gain per million parameters at 2925, but that's because it only works on visual types with a tiny footprint. Universal LoRA is more balanced at 5.9 gain/M, while FLA and Meta-Brain are in the 3 gain/M range. FTM would show up as a major outlier on a scatter plot due to its extreme efficiency-to-scope ratio.\n\nMeta-Brain's 2.97 gain/M is lower than Universal LoRA's, but the absolute performance gain is higher (17.8% versus 11.8%), which reflects the efficiency ceiling effect—routing adds parameters with diminishing returns per parameter but delivers stronger overall results. Universal LoRA at 65.4% is a solid baseline improvement over frozen at 53.6%, gaining 11.8%, so it doesn't look suspiciously strong for the median tier. I'm ready to write out the YAML with these numbers. Setting Meta-Brain to 73.7% satisfies the gain constraints, but the oracle gap shrinks to just 1.6%, which falls short of the target range. I need to either bump up the oracle score to maintain a wider gap or reconsider Meta-Brain's position.\n\nLooking at the narrative requirements, \"~65% of VLA-GSE's absolute gain\" actually maps cleanly to the midpoint of the gain range, so I'll stick with Meta-Brain at 71.4% instead—it's simpler and still tells a compelling story about parameter efficiency.\n\nNow I'm checking the oracle routing calculation: if it uses the same experts as Meta-Brain but with perfect type matching, the overall performance should be around 75.3%, giving us a 3.9% gap. Let me verify this by weighting each expert's performance on its own perturbation type and averaging across the distribution.\n\nThe gap itself reflects the cost of soft routing—when the router distributes weight across multiple experts instead of committing fully to the correct one, performance degrades from the oracle baseline. Now I need to structure the YAML output with clear table