{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015PWxYAKeEVDj456zMHzPJH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Routing by perturbation type yields clear but moderate gains, strongest on Visual and Physical where perturbations disrupt\n  localized processing stages. Spatial expert underperforms due to layout/distractor perturbations requiring whole-trajectory\n  reasoning beyond single-step LoRA correction. Universal LoRA is a surprisingly strong baseline (~65%) from mixed-training\n  cross-type robustness, narrowing the routing advantage. Frame Meta-Brain as parameter-efficient complement to capacity\n  routing: ~65% of VLA-GSE absolute gain at 5% parameters. Ablation ladder Frozen < Universal < Meta-Brain < Oracle is\n  clean and statistically significant. Add mechanistic analysis section explaining per-type routing benefit asymmetry —\n  turns partial spatial result into insight rather than weakness. Consider additional experiment combining Meta-Brain +\n  universal LoRA residual to show routing is additive to uniform adaptation.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"LoRA Expert Training — Specialized vs Universal\"\n    plan_id: plan_002\n    description: >\n      Train 4 rank-16 LoRA adapters on frozen pi0: LoRA-V (visual data), LoRA-S (spatial data),\n      LoRA-P (physical data), Universal (mixed all-type data). Evaluate each on all 7 perturbation\n      types to obtain the cross-type performance matrix.\n    expected_results:\n      lora_v_visual_avg: 78.3\n      lora_v_camera: 75.2\n      lora_v_light: 82.0\n      lora_v_background: 78.4\n      lora_v_noise: 77.6\n      lora_v_spatial_avg: 54.0\n      lora_v_physical: 50.6\n      lora_s_spatial_avg: 68.0\n      lora_s_distractor: 69.4\n      lora_s_layout: 66.6\n      lora_s_visual_avg: 46.9\n      lora_s_physical: 49.2\n      lora_p_physical: 78.0\n      lora_p_visual_avg: 47.9\n      lora_p_spatial_avg: 53.7\n      universal_overall: 65.4\n      universal_visual_avg: 65.35\n      universal_spatial_avg: 63.7\n      universal_physical: 69.0\n      specialization_delta_visual: 12.95\n      specialization_delta_spatial: 4.3\n      specialization_delta_physical: 9.0\n      specialization_delta_max: 12.95\n    result_rationale: >\n      Visual expert (LoRA-V) shows the strongest specialization (+13% over universal on visual types)\n      because visual perturbations (camera/light/background/noise) corrupt early PaliGemma representations\n      in a consistent, localized manner — rank-16 LoRA can learn a compact correction to these early-layer\n      distortions. Physical expert (LoRA-P) shows strong but smaller specialization (+9%) because robot\n      perturbations (changed dynamics/mass) affect action-space outputs which are more distributed across\n      layers. Spatial expert (LoRA-S) shows weak specialization (+4.3%) because layout and distractor\n      perturbations fundamentally change the required trajectory — not just a representational shift but\n      a different action sequence — and single-step LoRA correction at the representation level cannot\n      replan a trajectory. Cross-type performance degrades sharply (below frozen baseline) because\n      each specialized expert learns type-specific correction biases that actively harm representations\n      when applied to the wrong perturbation type. Universal LoRA at 65.4% is stronger than expected\n      because mixed training provides partial cross-type robustness — the LoRA learns an average\n      correction that modestly helps all types without hurting any.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Cross-Type Expert Performance Matrix (success rate %)\"\n        header: [Method, camera, light, background, noise, distractor, layout, robot, Overall]\n        rows:\n          - [Frozen pi0, 47.6, 56.2, 51.8, 49.4, 58.6, 57.8, 53.8, 53.6]\n          - [LoRA-V, 75.2, 82.0, 78.4, 77.6, 54.2, 53.8, 50.6, 67.4]\n          - [LoRA-S, 44.2, 50.4, 47.8, 45.0, 69.4, 66.6, 49.2, 53.2]\n          - [LoRA-P, 45.6, 51.2, 48.4, 46.2, 54.0, 53.4, 78.0, 53.8]\n          - [Universal, 62.8, 69.4, 66.2, 63.0, 64.8, 62.6, 69.0, 65.4]\n      - name: \"Specialization Delta (expert own-type minus universal same-type)\"\n        header: [Coarse Type, Expert Own-Type, Universal Same-Type, Delta]\n        rows:\n          - [Visual, 78.3, 65.35, +12.95]\n          - [Spatial, 68.0, 63.7, +4.3]\n          - [Physical, 78.0, 69.0, +9.0]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Heatmap (4 experts × 7 perturbation types) with success rates. Strong diagonal blocks\n        visible for LoRA-V (4 visual columns bright) and LoRA-P (robot column bright). LoRA-S\n        shows only moderate brightness on distractor/layout. Universal row uniformly mid-tone.\n        Off-diagonal cells for specialized experts are dark (below frozen baseline), visually\n        demonstrating the cost of mismatched routing.\n      - >\n        Grouped bar chart: 3 coarse types on x-axis, bars for [Frozen, Universal, Specialized Expert].\n        Visual group shows large gap between universal and LoRA-V. Physical group shows moderate gap.\n        Spatial group shows minimal gap. Frozen baseline as dashed horizontal line per group.\n    analysis: >\n      The cross-type matrix is the foundational evidence for why routing matters: correct expert\n      helps significantly (+9 to +13% over universal), wrong expert hurts severely (below frozen).\n      This asymmetry means random or poor routing can be worse than no routing at all, motivating\n      the need for accurate PAR classification. The weak spatial specialization is interpretable:\n      layout and distractor perturbations change the goal-relevant spatial configuration, requiring\n      trajectory-level replanning that a single-step representation correction cannot provide. This\n      is a fundamental limitation of the LoRA-based approach for spatial perturbations, not a routing\n      failure. The universal LoRA's surprising strength (65.4%) sets a high bar for Meta-Brain — the\n      routing system must do substantially better to justify 3× the parameters.\n\n  - name: \"Meta-Brain Assembly — PAR Router + LoRA Bank\"\n    plan_id: plan_003\n    description: >\n      Assemble PAR router (PaliGemma layer 6, 2-layer MLP, initialized from Phase 0 weights)\n      with 3 LoRA experts. Soft routing: h_corrected = h_frozen + Σ_k p(type_k) · LoRA_k(h_frozen).\n      End-to-end routing fine-tuning with frozen LoRA parameters (only tune PAR weights).\n      Evaluate on full LIBERO-Plus benchmark.\n    expected_results:\n      libero_plus_overall: 71.4\n      per_type_camera: 71.0\n      per_type_light: 77.6\n      per_type_background: 74.4\n      per_type_noise: 73.0\n      per_type_distractor: 65.2\n      per_type_layout: 64.4\n      per_type_robot: 74.2\n      visual_avg: 74.0\n      spatial_avg: 64.8\n      physical: 74.2\n      gain_over_frozen: 17.8\n      gain_over_universal: 6.0\n      routing_diag_visual: 0.82\n      routing_diag_spatial: 0.68\n      routing_diag_physical: 0.80\n      routing_diagonality_mean: 0.77\n      hard_assignment_accuracy: 89.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Soft routing dilutes the correction signal compared to hard (oracle) routing, costing ~4%\n      overall. The dilution is uniform across types (3-5% per type) because even a small weight\n      on the wrong expert introduces harmful correction biases. Spatial routing diagonality is\n      lowest (0.68) because layout/distractor perturbations share visual features with\n      camera/background changes at layer 6 — the PAR router's operating point. Despite this,\n      Meta-Brain still improves over universal on spatial (+1.1%) because even imperfect soft\n      routing toward LoRA-S is better than no type awareness. Visual gain (+8.65% over universal)\n      is large because high routing confidence (0.82) allows LoRA-V to dominate the correction.\n      Physical gain (+5.2%) is moderate due to good routing (0.80) but more distributed\n      perturbation effects. Hard assignment accuracy (89%) is lower than Phase 0 classification\n      (99.3%) because the router is fine-tuned for task success, not classification accuracy —\n      it learns to soften assignments when mixing provides marginal task benefit.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Meta-Brain Per-Perturbation Success Rate (%)\"\n        header: [Perturbation, Frozen, Universal, Meta-Brain, Gain over Universal]\n        rows:\n          - [camera, 47.6, 62.8, 71.0, +8.2]\n          - [light, 56.2, 69.4, 77.6, +8.2]\n          - [background, 51.8, 66.2, 74.4, +8.2]\n          - [noise, 49.4, 63.0, 73.0, +10.0]\n          - [distractor, 58.6, 64.8, 65.2, +0.4]\n          - [layout, 57.8, 62.6, 64.4, +1.8]\n          - [robot, 53.8, 69.0, 74.2, +5.2]\n          - [Overall, 53.6, 65.4, 71.4, +6.0]\n      - name: \"PAR Soft Routing Weight Matrix (mean weights per true type)\"\n        header: [True Type, P(LoRA-V), P(LoRA-S), P(LoRA-P)]\n        rows:\n          - [Visual, 0.82, 0.06, 0.12]\n          - [Spatial, 0.20, 0.68, 0.12]\n          - [Physical, 0.12, 0.08, 0.80]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Routing weight heatmap (3×3): rows = true perturbation type, columns = routing weight\n        to expert. Near-diagonal pattern visible but imperfect. Visual and Physical rows show\n        strong diagonal dominance (0.82, 0.80). Spatial row is more diffuse (0.68 diagonal,\n        0.20 leak to Visual). Annotated with actual weight values in each cell.\n      - >\n        Per-type success rate bar chart: 7 perturbation types on x-axis, grouped bars for\n        Frozen/Universal/Meta-Brain. Clear visual separation on camera/light/background/noise\n        (Meta-Brain >> Universal >> Frozen). Minimal separation on distractor/layout.\n        Moderate separation on robot.\n    analysis: >\n      Meta-Brain achieves the target ordering (Meta-Brain > Universal > Frozen on all 7 types)\n      but the margin varies dramatically: +8-10% on visual types, +5% on physical, only +0.4-1.8%\n      on spatial. This asymmetry is mechanistically interpretable: visual perturbations corrupt\n      the representation in a way that a type-specific LoRA can precisely invert, while spatial\n      perturbations require policy-level replanning beyond representation correction. The routing\n      weight analysis reveals the PAR router appropriately lacks confidence on spatial inputs\n      (diag 0.68), which limits both the benefit and the harm of routing on these types.\n      The end-to-end routing fine-tuning shifts the router from pure classification toward\n      task-success optimization, explaining the gap between Phase 0 accuracy (99.3%) and\n      deployed routing accuracy (~89%).\n\n  - name: \"Oracle Routing — Upper Bound for Routing Quality\"\n    plan_id: plan_004\n    description: >\n      Replace PAR soft router with ground-truth perturbation type labels (hard 1-hot routing\n      to the correct expert). Same LoRA experts from plan_002. Measures the ceiling for\n      routing quality and quantifies PAR's gap to perfect routing.\n    expected_results:\n      oracle_overall: 75.3\n      oracle_camera: 75.2\n      oracle_light: 82.0\n      oracle_background: 78.4\n      oracle_noise: 77.6\n      oracle_distractor: 69.4\n      oracle_layout: 66.6\n      oracle_robot: 78.0\n      oracle_par_gap_overall: 3.9\n      oracle_par_gap_visual: 4.3\n      oracle_par_gap_spatial: 3.8\n      oracle_par_gap_physical: 3.8\n    result_rationale: >\n      Oracle routing eliminates soft-routing dilution entirely — each perturbation type receives\n      100% correction from its matched expert, with zero contamination from mismatched experts.\n      The 3.9% overall gap from Meta-Brain to oracle quantifies the cost of learned soft routing\n      versus perfect hard routing. This gap arises from two sources: (1) non-zero weight on wrong\n      experts injects harmful correction biases (~60% of the gap), and (2) the router's lower\n      confidence on spatial perturbations causes LoRA-S to be under-weighted even on true spatial\n      inputs (~40% of the gap). The gap is moderate (not catastrophic), indicating PAR routing\n      is functional but not near-perfect. Closing this gap further would require either better\n      features for spatial classification at layer 6, or a hybrid routing scheme that uses hard\n      routing when confidence is high and universal fallback when confidence is low. Note that\n      oracle performance (75.3%) still trails VLA-GSE (81.2%) by 5.9%, confirming that the\n      performance ceiling is set by pi0 backbone capacity and LoRA rank, not routing quality.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Oracle vs PAR Per-Type Gap (%)\"\n        header: [Perturbation, Oracle, Meta-Brain (PAR), Gap]\n        rows:\n          - [camera, 75.2, 71.0, 4.2]\n          - [light, 82.0, 77.6, 4.4]\n          - [background, 78.4, 74.4, 4.0]\n          - [noise, 77.6, 73.0, 4.6]\n          - [distractor, 69.4, 65.2, 4.2]\n          - [layout, 66.6, 64.4, 2.2]\n          - [robot, 78.0, 74.2, 3.8]\n          - [Overall, 75.3, 71.4, 3.9]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Paired bar chart: for each perturbation type, side-by-side bars for Oracle (blue) and\n        Meta-Brain PAR (orange) with the gap annotated above. Layout has the smallest gap (2.2%)\n        because LoRA-S is already weak regardless of routing. Noise has the largest gap (4.6%)\n        because sensor noise is hardest for the router to confidently classify.\n    analysis: >\n      The oracle-PAR gap of 3.9% is in the moderate range — large enough to matter for absolute\n      performance but not large enough to invalidate the routing approach. The implication for\n      Claim 2 is important: even with perfect routing, the gain over universal LoRA would be\n      75.3 - 65.4 = 9.9%, only ~64% more than Meta-Brain's 6.0%. This means routing quality\n      accounts for ~40% of the potential routing benefit left on the table. For the paper,\n      frame this as: \"PAR captures ~60% of the theoretically available routing benefit,\n      with the remaining gap attributable to soft-routing dilution and spatial ambiguity.\"\n      The gap also establishes headroom for future router improvements (e.g., confidence-gated\n      hard routing).\n\n  - name: \"Ablation Ladder — Frozen → Universal → Meta-Brain → Oracle\"\n    plan_id: plan_005\n    description: >\n      Systematic 5-seed comparison on full LIBERO-Plus: Frozen pi0, Universal LoRA (rank 16, ~2M),\n      Meta-Brain (PAR + 3 LoRA, ~6M), Oracle routing (ground-truth type). Report overall and\n      per-perturbation breakdown with statistical significance. Core evidence for Claim 2.\n    expected_results:\n      frozen_mean: 53.6\n      frozen_std: 1.8\n      universal_mean: 65.4\n      universal_std: 2.1\n      meta_brain_mean: 71.4\n      meta_brain_std: 2.4\n      oracle_mean: 75.3\n      oracle_std: 1.9\n      gain_frozen_to_universal: 11.8\n      gain_universal_to_meta_brain: 6.0\n      gain_meta_brain_to_oracle: 3.9\n      pvalue_universal_gt_frozen: 0.0003\n      pvalue_meta_brain_gt_universal: 0.008\n      pvalue_oracle_gt_meta_brain: 0.021\n      meta_brain_gain_over_frozen: 17.8\n    result_rationale: >\n      The ablation ladder shows a clean monotonic ordering (Frozen < Universal < Meta-Brain < Oracle)\n      with all adjacent pairs statistically significant at p < 0.05. Step sizes decrease going up\n      the ladder: +11.8 (baseline adaptation), +6.0 (routing benefit), +3.9 (routing quality gap).\n      The largest step is Frozen → Universal, confirming that simply adding LoRA adaptation\n      captures most of the low-hanging fruit. The Universal → Meta-Brain step (+6.0%) is the\n      headline number for Claim 2 — routing by perturbation type provides meaningful additional\n      benefit beyond uniform adaptation, but the advantage is moderate (not dominant). The\n      diminishing step sizes reflect a fundamental pattern: each successive refinement addresses\n      a smaller remaining error surface. Standard deviations are 1.8-2.4%, typical for LIBERO-Plus\n      with per-seed task ordering variance. Meta-Brain's higher std (2.4%) reflects routing\n      sensitivity to seed-specific perturbation distributions.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Ablation Ladder — Overall Success Rate (%, 5 seeds)\"\n        header: [Method, Params, Mean, Std, 95% CI, p vs. Previous]\n        rows:\n          - [Frozen pi0, 0, 53.6, 1.8, \"[51.4, 55.8]\", \"—\"]\n          - [Universal LoRA, ~2M, 65.4, 2.1, \"[62.8, 68.0]\", \"<0.001\"]\n          - [Meta-Brain, ~6M, 71.4, 2.4, \"[68.4, 74.4]\", \"0.008\"]\n          - [Oracle, ~6M, 75.3, 1.9, \"[72.9, 77.7]\", \"0.021\"]\n      - name: \"Ablation Ladder — Per-Perturbation Breakdown (%, 5-seed mean)\"\n        header: [Method, camera, light, bg, noise, distr, layout, robot, Overall]\n        rows:\n          - [Frozen, 47.6, 56.2, 51.8, 49.4, 58.6, 57.8, 53.8, 53.6]\n          - [Universal, 62.8, 69.4, 66.2, 63.0, 64.8, 62.6, 69.0, 65.4]\n          - [Meta-Brain, 71.0, 77.6, 74.4, 73.0, 65.2, 64.4, 74.2, 71.4]\n          - [Oracle, 75.2, 82.0, 78.4, 77.6, 69.4, 66.6, 78.0, 75.3]\n      - name: \"Routing Gain Decomposition by Coarse Type\"\n        header: [Coarse Type, Universal, Meta-Brain, Routing Gain, \"% of Oracle Ceiling\"]\n        rows:\n          - [Visual (4 types), 65.35, 74.0, +8.65, \"66.8%\"]\n          - [Spatial (2 types), 63.7, 64.8, +1.1, \"25.6%\"]\n          - [Physical (1 type), 69.0, 74.2, +5.2, \"57.8%\"]\n          - [Overall, 65.4, 71.4, +6.0, \"60.6%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Stacked bar chart (the ablation ladder): 4 methods on x-axis, success rate on y-axis.\n        Bars are colored in a gradient from red (Frozen) to green (Oracle). Error bars show ±1 std.\n        Horizontal dashed lines at 81.2% (VLA-GSE reference) and 53.6% (Frozen baseline). Clear\n        monotonic increase with decreasing step sizes. Significance brackets between adjacent pairs.\n      - >\n        Per-perturbation grouped bar chart: 7 perturbation types on x-axis, 4 methods as grouped\n        bars. Reveals the asymmetric routing benefit — large visual/physical separation between\n        Universal and Meta-Brain bars, minimal spatial separation. Oracle bars highest across all\n        types but layout/distractor gap is notably small.\n    analysis: >\n      The ablation ladder is clean and publishable. All pairwise comparisons are significant.\n      The key narrative point is that Meta-Brain captures 60.6% of the theoretically available\n      routing benefit (oracle ceiling). The per-type decomposition reveals that routing works\n      best where perturbations are localized (visual: 66.8% of ceiling captured) and least\n      where perturbations are distributed (spatial: 25.6%). This naturally leads to the\n      mechanistic analysis section in the paper explaining which processing stages each\n      perturbation type disrupts. For Claim 2, the headline is: \"Routing by perturbation\n      type improves over universal LoRA by 6.0 ± 2.3% (p = 0.008), with gains concentrated\n      on perturbation types that disrupt localized processing stages.\" This reframing\n      adds nuance rather than claiming universal superiority.\n\n  - name: \"FTM/FLA Reproduction — Visual-Only Baseline\"\n    plan_id: plan_006\n    description: >\n      Reproduce FTM (4K params) and FLA (4.7M params) on pi0 for visual perturbation types.\n      Compare with LoRA-V and Meta-Brain to establish visual-only baselines and demonstrate\n      Meta-Brain's multi-type advantage.\n    expected_results:\n      ftm_camera: 69.8\n      ftm_light: 76.4\n      ftm_background: 72.6\n      ftm_noise: 68.2\n      ftm_visual_avg: 71.75\n      ftm_overall: 65.3\n      fla_camera: 73.8\n      fla_light: 80.2\n      fla_background: 76.8\n      fla_noise: 75.4\n      fla_visual_avg: 76.55\n      fla_overall: 68.1\n      lora_v_visual_avg: 78.3\n      meta_brain_visual_avg: 74.0\n      meta_brain_overall: 71.4\n      meta_brain_advantage_over_ftm_overall: 6.1\n      meta_brain_advantage_over_fla_overall: 3.3\n    result_rationale: >\n      FTM at 4K params is remarkably parameter-efficient for visual perturbations (71.75% visual avg)\n      but fundamentally cannot handle spatial/physical types, leaving those at frozen baseline levels\n      and yielding only 65.3% overall. FLA at 4.7M params approaches LoRA-V quality on visual types\n      (76.55% vs 78.3%) but also cannot generalize beyond visual. Meta-Brain's overall advantage\n      (71.4% vs 65.3-68.1%) comes entirely from spatial and physical perturbation handling. On\n      visual types alone, LoRA-V (78.3%) > FLA (76.55%) > Meta-Brain routed (74.0%) > FTM (71.75%),\n      showing that specialized visual methods can match or exceed routing-based correction for\n      visual perturbations specifically. The key message: FTM/FLA are excellent visual-only\n      solutions but cannot serve as general robustness methods. Meta-Brain's value is breadth\n      of coverage, not per-type optimality. FTM's per-parameter efficiency on visual types\n      (11.7%/0.004M = 2925%/M) dwarfs all other methods but is limited to a single perturbation\n      modality.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Visual Perturbation Methods Comparison (%)\"\n        header: [Method, Params, camera, light, bg, noise, Visual Avg, Overall]\n        rows:\n          - [FTM, 4K, 69.8, 76.4, 72.6, 68.2, 71.75, 65.3]\n          - [FLA, 4.7M, 73.8, 80.2, 76.8, 75.4, 76.55, 68.1]\n          - [LoRA-V, ~2M, 75.2, 82.0, 78.4, 77.6, 78.3, 67.4]\n          - [Meta-Brain, ~6M, 71.0, 77.6, 74.4, 73.0, 74.0, 71.4]\n          - [Universal, ~2M, 62.8, 69.4, 66.2, 63.0, 65.35, 65.4]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Two-panel figure. Left: visual-types-only comparison (4 visual perturbation bars per\n        method). LoRA-V tops, FLA close second, Meta-Brain third, FTM fourth, Universal last.\n        Right: overall LIBERO-Plus comparison. Meta-Brain tops (71.4%), FLA second (68.1%),\n        LoRA-V/Universal/FTM clustered (65-67%). Shows the reversal: best visual-only method\n        is not the best overall method.\n    analysis: >\n      This experiment establishes two key points for the paper: (1) Visual-only methods are\n      competitive on visual perturbations but fail as general robustness solutions — there is\n      no free lunch. (2) Meta-Brain's routing approach provides the best overall coverage by\n      handling all perturbation types, even though it doesn't maximize any single type. For\n      Claim 3, FTM serves as an extreme point on the parameter-efficiency scatter plot: incredible\n      visual-only efficiency but zero spatial/physical coverage. Meta-Brain occupies a qualitatively\n      different position: moderate efficiency with universal coverage.\n\n  - name: \"Parameter Efficiency Analysis\"\n    plan_id: plan_007\n    description: >\n      Compute gain-over-frozen per method, normalize by trainable parameters. Produce the\n      parameter efficiency scatter plot (x=params log scale, y=gain-over-baseline). Include\n      Meta-Brain, Universal LoRA, FTM, FLA, VLA-GSE (from paper, different backbone caveat).\n    expected_results:\n      meta_brain_gain: 17.8\n      meta_brain_params_M: 6\n      meta_brain_gain_per_M: 2.97\n      universal_gain: 11.8\n      universal_params_M: 2\n      universal_gain_per_M: 5.9\n      ftm_gain: 11.7\n      ftm_params_M: 0.004\n      ftm_gain_per_M: 2925.0\n      fla_gain: 14.5\n      fla_params_M: 4.7\n      fla_gain_per_M: 3.09\n      vla_gse_gain: 27.6\n      vla_gse_params_M: 114\n      vla_gse_gain_per_M: 0.24\n      meta_brain_pct_of_vla_gse_gain: 64.5\n      meta_brain_pct_of_vla_gse_params: 5.3\n    result_rationale: >\n      Meta-Brain achieves 2.97%/M gain-per-parameter, sitting in the middle of the efficiency\n      spectrum. Universal LoRA is the most efficient multi-type method (5.9%/M) but has a lower\n      absolute gain ceiling. FTM is an extreme outlier (2925%/M) demonstrating that ultra-compact\n      methods exist for single-type perturbations, but this efficiency doesn't scale to multi-type\n      coverage. VLA-GSE achieves the highest absolute gain (27.6%) but at 0.24%/M efficiency —\n      a 12× parameter overhead vs Meta-Brain for 55% more gain. The headline for Claim 3:\n      \"Meta-Brain achieves 64.5% of VLA-GSE's robustness improvement using 5.3% of its trainable\n      parameters, demonstrating perturbation-diagnostic routing as a parameter-efficient alternative\n      to capacity-based expert routing.\" The different-backbone caveat (pi0 vs Qwen3-VL-4B) applies\n      to absolute numbers but the efficiency ratio is still informative.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Parameter Efficiency Comparison\"\n        header: [Method, Backbone, Params (M), Gain over Frozen (%), Gain/M Params, Coverage]\n        rows:\n          - [FTM, pi0, 0.004, 11.7, 2925.0, Visual only]\n          - [Universal LoRA, pi0, 2, 11.8, 5.9, All types]\n          - [FLA, pi0, 4.7, 14.5, 3.09, Visual only]\n          - [Meta-Brain, pi0, 6, 17.8, 2.97, All types]\n          - [VLA-GSE†, Qwen3-VL-4B, 114, 27.6, 0.24, All types]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Scatter plot: x-axis = trainable parameters (log scale, 0.001M to 200M), y-axis = gain\n        over frozen baseline (%). Each method is a labeled point with marker size proportional to\n        coverage (all-type methods larger). FTM is far left (tiny params, moderate gain). Universal\n        LoRA and Meta-Brain are center-left. VLA-GSE is far right (high gain, huge params). A\n        Pareto frontier line connects FTM → Universal → Meta-Brain, showing that Meta-Brain extends\n        the efficiency frontier beyond universal adaptation. VLA-GSE sits above the frontier but\n        at 19× the cost. Dashed diagonal lines show iso-efficiency contours (%/M). Annotation:\n        \"Meta-Brain: 65% of VLA-GSE gain at 5% parameters.\" VLA-GSE point marked with † for\n        different backbone.\n    analysis: >\n      The scatter plot is the visual centerpiece of Claim 3. The key insight is that Meta-Brain\n      extends the Pareto frontier established by Universal LoRA — it proves that routing-based\n      specialization extracts more gain per parameter than simply increasing uniform LoRA rank.\n      The framing should be \"complementary to capacity routing, not replacement\": Meta-Brain\n      operates in a different region of the parameter-efficiency space than VLA-GSE. For\n      resource-constrained deployment (edge devices, real-time control), Meta-Brain offers\n      a practical operating point. An ideal system might compose both: Meta-Brain for\n      type-aware correction + capacity routing for residual error.\n\n  - name: \"Correction Memory Ablation — With/Without CM\"\n    plan_id: plan_008\n    description: >\n      Compare Meta-Brain with and without Correction Memory module. CM modulates routing\n      weights using retrieved exemplars from a FAISS-indexed memory bank. Quantify marginal\n      gain overall and per perturbation type.\n    expected_results:\n      meta_brain_no_cm: 71.4\n      meta_brain_with_cm: 72.6\n      cm_ablation_delta: 1.2\n      cm_delta_visual: 0.8\n      cm_delta_spatial: 0.4\n      cm_delta_physical: 2.8\n      cm_alpha_learned: 0.23\n      cm_retrieval_latency_ms: 1.4\n    result_rationale: >\n      CM provides a marginal +1.2% overall gain, concentrated on physical perturbations (+2.8%)\n      where the same robot dynamics changes recur consistently across episodes — exemplar\n      retrieval identifies \"I've seen this perturbation before\" and sharpens the routing weight\n      toward LoRA-P. Visual gain is small (+0.8%) because the PAR router already routes visual\n      perturbations confidently (diag 0.82) and CM's modulation has little room to improve.\n      Spatial gain is minimal (+0.4%) because spatial perturbations are fundamentally different\n      each time (different object positions) so retrieved exemplars provide weak signal. The\n      learned alpha parameter (0.23) confirms the system's own assessment: CM contributes\n      ~23% weighting to the final routing decision, indicating it's a secondary correction to\n      PAR, not a primary signal. The 1.4ms retrieval latency adds negligible overhead to the\n      10Hz control loop. CM is worth including as an optional module but does not change\n      the core story.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Correction Memory Ablation (%)\"\n        header: [Configuration, Visual, Spatial, Physical, Overall]\n        rows:\n          - [Meta-Brain (no CM), 74.0, 64.8, 74.2, 71.4]\n          - [Meta-Brain + CM, 74.8, 65.2, 77.0, 72.6]\n          - [CM Delta, +0.8, +0.4, +2.8, +1.2]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Grouped bar chart: 3 coarse types + overall on x-axis, paired bars (with/without CM).\n        Physical bar shows visible CM benefit. Visual and spatial bars nearly identical.\n        Error bars from 3 seeds. The difference is within 1 std for visual/spatial but\n        marginally significant for physical.\n    analysis: >\n      CM's marginal contribution (1.2%) is expected in the median tier. It validates the module\n      as a sound idea (positive delta on all types) but not a paper-defining contribution.\n      Present CM as \"optional module, +1.2% overall, primarily benefiting recurrent physical\n      perturbations.\" The per-type pattern adds a small mechanistic insight: exemplar-based\n      routing modulation works best when perturbations are consistent (physics changes) rather\n      than combinatorially varied (spatial layouts). For the paper, CM goes in the ablation\n      section with 1-2 sentences, not a dedicated section.\n\n  - name: \"Router Architecture Comparison — MLP vs Linear vs Attention\"\n    plan_id: plan_009\n    description: >\n      Compare PAR router variants on PaliGemma layer 6: (a) 2-layer MLP (default, ~50K params),\n      (b) linear classifier (~6K params), (c) cross-attention router (~120K params). Evaluate\n      both routing accuracy and downstream task success to test whether classification accuracy\n      improvements translate to task performance.\n    expected_results:\n      mlp_routing_accuracy: 93.8\n      mlp_task_success: 71.4\n      mlp_params: 50000\n      linear_routing_accuracy: 90.6\n      linear_task_success: 70.2\n      linear_params: 6000\n      attention_routing_accuracy: 94.2\n      attention_task_success: 71.2\n      attention_params: 120000\n      mlp_vs_linear_task_gap: 1.2\n      mlp_vs_attention_task_gap: 0.2\n    result_rationale: >\n      MLP (default) achieves 93.8% routing accuracy and 71.4% task success, offering the best\n      cost/performance tradeoff. Linear router loses 3.2% routing accuracy, which translates to\n      a modest 1.2% task success drop — the per-token representation at layer 6 is already\n      quite separable (Phase 0 showed F1=0.993 with MLP), so a linear boundary captures most\n      of the structure but misses some nonlinear decision boundaries near cluster edges.\n      Attention router adds 0.4% routing accuracy over MLP (the classes are well-separated\n      enough that attention over token sequences adds minimal information beyond mean pooling)\n      but this doesn't translate to task success improvement (71.2% vs 71.4%) — the 0.4%\n      classification gain falls within the noise floor of rollout evaluation. The attention\n      router's 2.4× parameter overhead is unjustified. Conclusion: MLP is the right choice,\n      confirming the Phase 0 finding that perturbation type is a simple classification problem\n      at layer 6.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Router Architecture Comparison\"\n        header: [Router, Params, Routing Acc (%), Task Success (%), Inference Overhead (ms)]\n        rows:\n          - [Linear, 6K, 90.6, 70.2, 0.02]\n          - [MLP (default), 50K, 93.8, 71.4, 0.05]\n          - [Cross-Attention, 120K, 94.2, 71.2, 0.18]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Dual-axis scatter: x = routing classification accuracy (%), left y = task success (%),\n        right y = router parameter count. Three points (Linear, MLP, Attention). Shows that\n        routing accuracy improvements beyond ~93% yield diminishing task success returns. The\n        gap between routing accuracy and task success is largest for Attention, illustrating\n        the disconnect between classification metrics and control metrics.\n    analysis: >\n      This experiment addresses reviewer concern C1 (\"Phase 0 classification is too easy\") from\n      a different angle: yes, the classification is relatively easy (even a linear classifier gets\n      90.6%), but the task-success gap between 90.6% and 93.8% routing accuracy is 1.2% —\n      confirming that routing accuracy does matter at the margins. The MLP's 3.2% classification\n      advantage over linear translates to 1.2% task success gain, roughly 1:0.37 ratio. Beyond\n      93.8%, additional classification gains have near-zero marginal task benefit. This helps\n      calibrate the oracle-PAR gap analysis: the 3.9% task gap from oracle routing quality\n      corresponds to moving from ~93.8% to ~100% classification accuracy, and the nonlinear\n      mapping suggests diminishing returns as routing approaches perfection.\n\n  - name: \"6-Class vs 3-Class Routing Comparison\"\n    plan_id: plan_010\n    description: >\n      Compare fine-grained 6-class routing (camera/light/background/noise/layout/robot, 6 LoRA\n      experts ~12M params) with coarse 3-class routing (Visual/Spatial/Physical, 3 experts ~6M).\n      Tests whether finer-grained perturbation-type routing justifies additional parameters and\n      architectural complexity.\n    expected_results:\n      three_class_overall: 71.4\n      three_class_routing_accuracy: 93.8\n      three_class_params_M: 6\n      six_class_overall: 72.2\n      six_class_routing_accuracy: 89.4\n      six_class_params_M: 12\n      delta_overall: 0.8\n      six_class_camera: 72.8\n      six_class_light: 79.2\n      six_class_background: 76.0\n      six_class_noise: 74.6\n      six_class_distractor: 66.0\n      six_class_layout: 65.2\n      six_class_robot: 81.6\n    result_rationale: >\n      6-class routing provides only +0.8% overall improvement over 3-class while doubling the\n      parameter budget (12M vs 6M) and dropping routing accuracy by 4.4% (89.4% vs 93.8%).\n      The marginal gain arises primarily from the robot expert (+3.8% from dedicated robot LoRA\n      vs shared Physical LoRA) where the 1-type Physical class benefits from fully dedicated\n      parameters. Intra-Visual perturbation types (camera/light/background/noise) do NOT benefit\n      significantly from separate experts because they share the same failure mode (early\n      representation corruption) — Phase 0 6-class analysis already showed high intra-Visual\n      similarity. The spatial split (distractor/layout) gains marginally because both types\n      require trajectory-level reasoning that LoRA cannot provide. The 4.4% routing accuracy\n      drop with 6 classes partially offsets any per-expert specialization gain: more classes\n      means more routing errors, and each error routes to a more narrowly specialized (thus\n      more harmful when mismatched) expert. Net assessment: 3-class is the better design point.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"6-Class vs 3-Class Comparison (%)\"\n        header: [Configuration, Classes, Params, Routing Acc, Overall, Δ vs 3-class]\n        rows:\n          - [3-class Meta-Brain, 3, ~6M, 93.8, 71.4, \"—\"]\n          - [6-class Meta-Brain, 6, ~12M, 89.4, 72.2, +0.8]\n      - name: \"6-Class Per-Type Breakdown vs 3-Class (%)\"\n        header: [Perturbation, 3-class, 6-class, Delta]\n        rows:\n          - [camera, 71.0, 72.8, +1.8]\n          - [light, 77.6, 79.2, +1.6]\n          - [background, 74.4, 76.0, +1.6]\n          - [noise, 73.0, 74.6, +1.6]\n          - [distractor, 65.2, 66.0, +0.8]\n          - [layout, 64.4, 65.2, +0.8]\n          - [robot, 74.2, 81.6, +7.4]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Stacked cost-benefit bar: left bar = 3-class (6M params, 71.4% success), right bar =\n        6-class (12M params, 72.2% success). The 2× parameter cost is visually dominant for a\n        barely visible success rate gain. Annotation: \"+0.8% for 2× params = poor ROI.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This directly addresses reviewer concern C5 (\"3-class taxonomy may be too coarse\"). The\n      answer is: 3-class is nearly optimal for this architecture. The 6-class robot improvement\n      (+7.4%) is notable in isolation but doesn't offset the systemic costs of finer routing.\n      The Phase 0 intra-Visual similarity finding (6-class accuracy 99.82% but intra-Visual\n      confusion is near-zero) predicted this outcome: types that cluster together in activation\n      space don't need separate experts. Include this as a 1-paragraph ablation result: \"Finer\n      6-class routing adds +0.8% at 2× parameters, confirming that the 3-class taxonomy aligns\n      with the mechanistic distinction in VLA processing stages.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cross-Backbone Validation — SmolVLA\"\n    plan_id: plan_011\n    description: >\n      Replicate Phase 0 classification + Phase 1 routing pipeline on SmolVLA to test whether\n      perturbation-diagnostic routing generalizes beyond pi0. SmolVLA uses a different vision\n      encoder (SigLIP) and language backbone (SmolLM), testing architecture independence.\n    expected_results:\n      smolvla_phase0_3class_f1: 0.938\n      smolvla_phase0_best_layer: 4\n      smolvla_frozen_baseline: 46.2\n      smolvla_universal_lora: 56.8\n      smolvla_meta_brain: 62.4\n      smolvla_gain_over_frozen: 16.2\n      smolvla_gain_over_universal: 5.6\n      smolvla_routing_diagonality: 0.73\n      smolvla_visual_specialization_delta: 10.4\n      smolvla_spatial_specialization_delta: 2.8\n      smolvla_physical_specialization_delta: 7.6\n    result_rationale: >\n      SmolVLA confirms the core finding: frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type\n      (F1=0.938, lower than pi0's 0.993 due to SigLIP's different feature structure but still\n      high). The best classification layer shifts to layer 4 (SmolVLA has fewer layers than\n      PaliGemma). Meta-Brain on SmolVLA achieves +16.2% gain over frozen (comparable to pi0's\n      +17.8%) and +5.6% over universal (comparable to pi0's +6.0%), demonstrating that the\n      routing benefit is architecture-independent. Absolute numbers are lower (62.4% vs 71.4%)\n      due to SmolVLA's weaker backbone capacity (SmolLM-360M vs pi0's larger model). The\n      per-type specialization pattern replicates: visual shows the largest delta (10.4%),\n      physical moderate (7.6%), spatial weak (2.8%). Spatial underperformance persists across\n      backbones, confirming it's a fundamental limitation of single-step LoRA correction for\n      trajectory-level perturbations, not an artifact of pi0's architecture. Routing diagonality\n      (0.73) is slightly lower than pi0 (0.77), reflecting SigLIP's less structured intermediate\n      representations for perturbation classification.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Cross-Backbone Comparison (pi0 vs SmolVLA)\"\n        header: [Metric, pi0, SmolVLA]\n        rows:\n          - [Phase 0 3-class F1, 0.993, 0.938]\n          - [Best classification layer, 6, 4]\n          - [Frozen baseline (%), 53.6, 46.2]\n          - [Universal LoRA (%), 65.4, 56.8]\n          - [Meta-Brain (%), 71.4, 62.4]\n          - [Gain over frozen (%), 17.8, 16.2]\n          - [Gain over universal (%), 6.0, 5.6]\n          - [Routing diagonality, 0.77, 0.73]\n          - [Visual specialization delta (%), 12.95, 10.4]\n          - [Spatial specialization delta (%), 4.3, 2.8]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Side-by-side ablation ladders for pi0 and SmolVLA. Both show the same monotonic\n        ordering (Frozen < Universal < Meta-Brain) with similar step sizes. Pi0 bars are\n        uniformly higher (stronger backbone) but the relative gains are comparable. Annotation\n        highlights the consistent routing benefit across architectures.\n    analysis: >\n      This experiment addresses reviewer concern C3 (\"single backbone, limited generalizability\").\n      The result is clearly positive: the qualitative pattern replicates exactly (same per-type\n      specialization ranking, same ablation ordering, comparable relative gains). The Phase 0\n      F1 drop (0.993 → 0.938) is expected — SigLIP and PaliGemma have different feature\n      structures — but 0.938 is still high enough for effective routing. The spatial\n      underperformance replication across backbones strengthens the mechanistic interpretation:\n      it's a property of the perturbation type, not the backbone. For the paper, include as\n      a validation subsection: \"Cross-backbone results on SmolVLA replicate the qualitative\n      findings, with comparable routing gains (+5.6% vs +6.0% over universal), confirming\n      that perturbation-diagnostic routing is an architecture-independent principle.\"\n      This is a P2 experiment — include if time/GPU budget permits, otherwise cite the\n      Phase 0 cross-backbone evidence from SAFE/probing literature.\n\n  - name: \"LoRA Layer Placement Ablation\"\n    plan_id: plan_012\n    description: >\n      Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA: (a) early (2-6), (b) mid (6-12), (c) late\n      (12-16), (d) all layers. Test hypothesis that perturbation types optimally correct at\n      different processing stages.\n    expected_results:\n      lora_v_early: 80.2\n      lora_v_mid: 74.8\n      lora_v_late: 71.4\n      lora_v_all: 78.3\n      lora_s_early: 64.6\n      lora_s_mid: 70.2\n      lora_s_late: 66.8\n      lora_s_all: 68.0\n      lora_p_early: 72.8\n      lora_p_mid: 75.2\n      lora_p_late: 80.6\n      lora_p_all: 78.0\n      optimal_placement_v: \"early (2-6)\"\n      optimal_placement_s: \"mid (6-12)\"\n      optimal_placement_p: \"late (12-16)\"\n      optimal_combined_overall: 73.8\n      all_layer_combined_overall: 71.4\n      layer_placement_gain: 2.4\n    result_rationale: >\n      The layer placement results support the mechanistic hypothesis with moderate effect sizes.\n      LoRA-V benefits most from early layers (+1.9% over all-layer) because visual perturbations\n      (camera/light/background/noise) corrupt the initial visual encoding in PaliGemma's early\n      layers — correcting at the source prevents error propagation. LoRA-S benefits from mid\n      layers (+2.2% over all-layer) because spatial reasoning (object relationships, layout\n      understanding) is computed in middle transformer layers where multi-head attention\n      integrates spatial context — though the absolute improvement remains modest due to the\n      fundamental trajectory-level limitation. LoRA-P benefits from late layers (+2.6% over\n      all-layer) because physical perturbations manifest at the action prediction stage in\n      late layers where dynamics models are implicitly computed. All-layer placement performs\n      as a reasonable default (second-best for each expert type) because the LoRA rank is\n      distributed across layers that contribute varying amounts. The combined optimal placement\n      (type-specific layers per expert) yields 73.8% overall (+2.4% over uniform all-layer\n      71.4%), suggesting layer-aware routing is a promising extension.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Layer Placement × Expert Type (own-type success rate %)\"\n        header: [Layer Range, LoRA-V (Visual), LoRA-S (Spatial), LoRA-P (Physical)]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Early (2-6)\", 80.2, 64.6, 72.8]\n          - [\"Mid (6-12)\", 74.8, 70.2, 75.2]\n          - [\"Late (12-16)\", 71.4, 66.8, 80.6]\n          - [\"All layers\", 78.3, 68.0, 78.0]\n      - name: \"Optimal Layer Placement Summary\"\n        header: [Expert, Best Layers, Own-Type Success, Δ vs All-Layer]\n        rows:\n          - [LoRA-V, \"Early (2-6)\", 80.2, +1.9]\n          - [LoRA-S, \"Mid (6-12)\", 70.2, +2.2]\n          - [LoRA-P, \"Late (12-16)\", 80.6, +2.6]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        3-panel line chart: one panel per expert type. X-axis = layer range (early/mid/late/all),\n        y-axis = own-type success rate. Each panel shows a different peak: LoRA-V peaks early,\n        LoRA-S peaks mid, LoRA-P peaks late. All-layer performance marked with a dashed horizontal\n        line in each panel for reference. The crossed peak pattern visually demonstrates that\n        different perturbation types optimally correct at different processing stages.\n    analysis: >\n      This is a P2 experiment that adds mechanistic depth but is not essential for the core\n      claims. The crossed peak pattern (V-early, S-mid, P-late) aligns with how VLA processing\n      stages work: visual encoding → spatial reasoning → action prediction. This provides\n      evidence for the paper's framing that perturbation types disrupt different processing\n      stages. The +2.4% gain from optimal layer placement is small but suggests a natural\n      extension: layer-aware routing that not only selects the right expert but applies it\n      at the right layers. This could close part of the oracle-PAR gap (3.9%) without\n      improving classification accuracy. For the paper, include as a supplementary analysis\n      with the crossed-peak figure — it's visually compelling and mechanistically interpretive.\n      Do not pursue layer-specific routing as a primary contribution; it's a natural future\n      work direction.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11289,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11289},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b5927951-3a29-4d8c-8713-a1fb502a4f6d","uuid":"9c3f1251-e668-4898-9a28-29aee78f87b4"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":762388,"duration_api_ms":763861,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Routing by perturbation type yields clear but moderate gains, strongest on Visual and Physical where perturbations disrupt\n  localized processing stages. Spatial expert underperforms due to layout/distractor perturbations requiring whole-trajectory\n  reasoning beyond single-step LoRA correction. Universal LoRA is a surprisingly strong baseline (~65%) from mixed-training\n  cross-type robustness, narrowing the routing advantage. Frame Meta-Brain as parameter-efficient complement to capacity\n  routing: ~65% of VLA-GSE absolute gain at 5% parameters. Ablation ladder Frozen < Universal < Meta-Brain < Oracle is\n  clean and statistically significant. Add mechanistic analysis section explaining per-type routing benefit asymmetry —\n  turns partial spatial result into insight rather than weakness. Consider additional experiment combining Meta-Brain +\n  universal LoRA residual to show routing is additive to uniform adaptation.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"LoRA Expert Training — Specialized vs Universal\"\n    plan_id: plan_002\n    description: >\n      Train 4 rank-16 LoRA adapters on frozen pi0: LoRA-V (visual data), LoRA-S (spatial data),\n      LoRA-P (physical data), Universal (mixed all-type data). Evaluate each on all 7 perturbation\n      types to obtain the cross-type performance matrix.\n    expected_results:\n      lora_v_visual_avg: 78.3\n      lora_v_camera: 75.2\n      lora_v_light: 82.0\n      lora_v_background: 78.4\n      lora_v_noise: 77.6\n      lora_v_spatial_avg: 54.0\n      lora_v_physical: 50.6\n      lora_s_spatial_avg: 68.0\n      lora_s_distractor: 69.4\n      lora_s_layout: 66.6\n      lora_s_visual_avg: 46.9\n      lora_s_physical: 49.2\n      lora_p_physical: 78.0\n      lora_p_visual_avg: 47.9\n      lora_p_spatial_avg: 53.7\n      universal_overall: 65.4\n      universal_visual_avg: 65.35\n      universal_spatial_avg: 63.7\n      universal_physical: 69.0\n      specialization_delta_visual: 12.95\n      specialization_delta_spatial: 4.3\n      specialization_delta_physical: 9.0\n      specialization_delta_max: 12.95\n    result_rationale: >\n      Visual expert (LoRA-V) shows the strongest specialization (+13% over universal on visual types)\n      because visual perturbations (camera/light/background/noise) corrupt early PaliGemma representations\n      in a consistent, localized manner — rank-16 LoRA can learn a compact correction to these early-layer\n      distortions. Physical expert (LoRA-P) shows strong but smaller specialization (+9%) because robot\n      perturbations (changed dynamics/mass) affect action-space outputs which are more distributed across\n      layers. Spatial expert (LoRA-S) shows weak specialization (+4.3%) because layout and distractor\n      perturbations fundamentally change the required trajectory — not just a representational shift but\n      a different action sequence — and single-step LoRA correction at the representation level cannot\n      replan a trajectory. Cross-type performance degrades sharply (below frozen baseline) because\n      each specialized expert learns type-specific correction biases that actively harm representations\n      when applied to the wrong perturbation type. Universal LoRA at 65.4% is stronger than expected\n      because mixed training provides partial cross-type robustness — the LoRA learns an average\n      correction that modestly helps all types without hurting any.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Cross-Type Expert Performance Matrix (success rate %)\"\n        header: [Method, camera, light, background, noise, distractor, layout, robot, Overall]\n        rows:\n          - [Frozen pi0, 47.6, 56.2, 51.8, 49.4, 58.6, 57.8, 53.8, 53.6]\n          - [LoRA-V, 75.2, 82.0, 78.4, 77.6, 54.2, 53.8, 50.6, 67.4]\n          - [LoRA-S, 44.2, 50.4, 47.8, 45.0, 69.4, 66.6, 49.2, 53.2]\n          - [LoRA-P, 45.6, 51.2, 48.4, 46.2, 54.0, 53.4, 78.0, 53.8]\n          - [Universal, 62.8, 69.4, 66.2, 63.0, 64.8, 62.6, 69.0, 65.4]\n      - name: \"Specialization Delta (expert own-type minus universal same-type)\"\n        header: [Coarse Type, Expert Own-Type, Universal Same-Type, Delta]\n        rows:\n          - [Visual, 78.3, 65.35, +12.95]\n          - [Spatial, 68.0, 63.7, +4.3]\n          - [Physical, 78.0, 69.0, +9.0]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Heatmap (4 experts × 7 perturbation types) with success rates. Strong diagonal blocks\n        visible for LoRA-V (4 visual columns bright) and LoRA-P (robot column bright). LoRA-S\n        shows only moderate brightness on distractor/layout. Universal row uniformly mid-tone.\n        Off-diagonal cells for specialized experts are dark (below frozen baseline), visually\n        demonstrating the cost of mismatched routing.\n      - >\n        Grouped bar chart: 3 coarse types on x-axis, bars for [Frozen, Universal, Specialized Expert].\n        Visual group shows large gap between universal and LoRA-V. Physical group shows moderate gap.\n        Spatial group shows minimal gap. Frozen baseline as dashed horizontal line per group.\n    analysis: >\n      The cross-type matrix is the foundational evidence for why routing matters: correct expert\n      helps significantly (+9 to +13% over universal), wrong expert hurts severely (below frozen).\n      This asymmetry means random or poor routing can be worse than no routing at all, motivating\n      the need for accurate PAR classification. The weak spatial specialization is interpretable:\n      layout and distractor perturbations change the goal-relevant spatial configuration, requiring\n      trajectory-level replanning that a single-step representation correction cannot provide. This\n      is a fundamental limitation of the LoRA-based approach for spatial perturbations, not a routing\n      failure. The universal LoRA's surprising strength (65.4%) sets a high bar for Meta-Brain — the\n      routing system must do substantially better to justify 3× the parameters.\n\n  - name: \"Meta-Brain Assembly — PAR Router + LoRA Bank\"\n    plan_id: plan_003\n    description: >\n      Assemble PAR router (PaliGemma layer 6, 2-layer MLP, initialized from Phase 0 weights)\n      with 3 LoRA experts. Soft routing: h_corrected = h_frozen + Σ_k p(type_k) · LoRA_k(h_frozen).\n      End-to-end routing fine-tuning with frozen LoRA parameters (only tune PAR weights).\n      Evaluate on full LIBERO-Plus benchmark.\n    expected_results:\n      libero_plus_overall: 71.4\n      per_type_camera: 71.0\n      per_type_light: 77.6\n      per_type_background: 74.4\n      per_type_noise: 73.0\n      per_type_distractor: 65.2\n      per_type_layout: 64.4\n      per_type_robot: 74.2\n      visual_avg: 74.0\n      spatial_avg: 64.8\n      physical: 74.2\n      gain_over_frozen: 17.8\n      gain_over_universal: 6.0\n      routing_diag_visual: 0.82\n      routing_diag_spatial: 0.68\n      routing_diag_physical: 0.80\n      routing_diagonality_mean: 0.77\n      hard_assignment_accuracy: 89.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Soft routing dilutes the correction signal compared to hard (oracle) routing, costing ~4%\n      overall. The dilution is uniform across types (3-5% per type) because even a small weight\n      on the wrong expert introduces harmful correction biases. Spatial routing diagonality is\n      lowest (0.68) because layout/distractor perturbations share visual features with\n      camera/background changes at layer 6 — the PAR router's operating point. Despite this,\n      Meta-Brain still improves over universal on spatial (+1.1%) because even imperfect soft\n      routing toward LoRA-S is better than no type awareness. Visual gain (+8.65% over universal)\n      is large because high routing confidence (0.82) allows LoRA-V to dominate the correction.\n      Physical gain (+5.2%) is moderate due to good routing (0.80) but more distributed\n      perturbation effects. Hard assignment accuracy (89%) is lower than Phase 0 classification\n      (99.3%) because the router is fine-tuned for task success, not classification accuracy —\n      it learns to soften assignments when mixing provides marginal task benefit.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Meta-Brain Per-Perturbation Success Rate (%)\"\n        header: [Perturbation, Frozen, Universal, Meta-Brain, Gain over Universal]\n        rows:\n          - [camera, 47.6, 62.8, 71.0, +8.2]\n          - [light, 56.2, 69.4, 77.6, +8.2]\n          - [background, 51.8, 66.2, 74.4, +8.2]\n          - [noise, 49.4, 63.0, 73.0, +10.0]\n          - [distractor, 58.6, 64.8, 65.2, +0.4]\n          - [layout, 57.8, 62.6, 64.4, +1.8]\n          - [robot, 53.8, 69.0, 74.2, +5.2]\n          - [Overall, 53.6, 65.4, 71.4, +6.0]\n      - name: \"PAR Soft Routing Weight Matrix (mean weights per true type)\"\n        header: [True Type, P(LoRA-V), P(LoRA-S), P(LoRA-P)]\n        rows:\n          - [Visual, 0.82, 0.06, 0.12]\n          - [Spatial, 0.20, 0.68, 0.12]\n          - [Physical, 0.12, 0.08, 0.80]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Routing weight heatmap (3×3): rows = true perturbation type, columns = routing weight\n        to expert. Near-diagonal pattern visible but imperfect. Visual and Physical rows show\n        strong diagonal dominance (0.82, 0.80). Spatial row is more diffuse (0.68 diagonal,\n        0.20 leak to Visual). Annotated with actual weight values in each cell.\n      - >\n        Per-type success rate bar chart: 7 perturbation types on x-axis, grouped bars for\n        Frozen/Universal/Meta-Brain. Clear visual separation on camera/light/background/noise\n        (Meta-Brain >> Universal >> Frozen). Minimal separation on distractor/layout.\n        Moderate separation on robot.\n    analysis: >\n      Meta-Brain achieves the target ordering (Meta-Brain > Universal > Frozen on all 7 types)\n      but the margin varies dramatically: +8-10% on visual types, +5% on physical, only +0.4-1.8%\n      on spatial. This asymmetry is mechanistically interpretable: visual perturbations corrupt\n      the representation in a way that a type-specific LoRA can precisely invert, while spatial\n      perturbations require policy-level replanning beyond representation correction. The routing\n      weight analysis reveals the PAR router appropriately lacks confidence on spatial inputs\n      (diag 0.68), which limits both the benefit and the harm of routing on these types.\n      The end-to-end routing fine-tuning shifts the router from pure classification toward\n      task-success optimization, explaining the gap between Phase 0 accuracy (99.3%) and\n      deployed routing accuracy (~89%).\n\n  - name: \"Oracle Routing — Upper Bound for Routing Quality\"\n    plan_id: plan_004\n    description: >\n      Replace PAR soft router with ground-truth perturbation type labels (hard 1-hot routing\n      to the correct expert). Same LoRA experts from plan_002. Measures the ceiling for\n      routing quality and quantifies PAR's gap to perfect routing.\n    expected_results:\n      oracle_overall: 75.3\n      oracle_camera: 75.2\n      oracle_light: 82.0\n      oracle_background: 78.4\n      oracle_noise: 77.6\n      oracle_distractor: 69.4\n      oracle_layout: 66.6\n      oracle_robot: 78.0\n      oracle_par_gap_overall: 3.9\n      oracle_par_gap_visual: 4.3\n      oracle_par_gap_spatial: 3.8\n      oracle_par_gap_physical: 3.8\n    result_rationale: >\n      Oracle routing eliminates soft-routing dilution entirely — each perturbation type receives\n      100% correction from its matched expert, with zero contamination from mismatched experts.\n      The 3.9% overall gap from Meta-Brain to oracle quantifies the cost of learned soft routing\n      versus perfect hard routing. This gap arises from two sources: (1) non-zero weight on wrong\n      experts injects harmful correction biases (~60% of the gap), and (2) the router's lower\n      confidence on spatial perturbations causes LoRA-S to be under-weighted even on true spatial\n      inputs (~40% of the gap). The gap is moderate (not catastrophic), indicating PAR routing\n      is functional but not near-perfect. Closing this gap further would require either better\n      features for spatial classification at layer 6, or a hybrid routing scheme that uses hard\n      routing when confidence is high and universal fallback when confidence is low. Note that\n      oracle performance (75.3%) still trails VLA-GSE (81.2%) by 5.9%, confirming that the\n      performance ceiling is set by pi0 backbone capacity and LoRA rank, not routing quality.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Oracle vs PAR Per-Type Gap (%)\"\n        header: [Perturbation, Oracle, Meta-Brain (PAR), Gap]\n        rows:\n          - [camera, 75.2, 71.0, 4.2]\n          - [light, 82.0, 77.6, 4.4]\n          - [background, 78.4, 74.4, 4.0]\n          - [noise, 77.6, 73.0, 4.6]\n          - [distractor, 69.4, 65.2, 4.2]\n          - [layout, 66.6, 64.4, 2.2]\n          - [robot, 78.0, 74.2, 3.8]\n          - [Overall, 75.3, 71.4, 3.9]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Paired bar chart: for each perturbation type, side-by-side bars for Oracle (blue) and\n        Meta-Brain PAR (orange) with the gap annotated above. Layout has the smallest gap (2.2%)\n        because LoRA-S is already weak regardless of routing. Noise has the largest gap (4.6%)\n        because sensor noise is hardest for the router to confidently classify.\n    analysis: >\n      The oracle-PAR gap of 3.9% is in the moderate range — large enough to matter for absolute\n      performance but not large enough to invalidate the routing approach. The implication for\n      Claim 2 is important: even with perfect routing, the gain over universal LoRA would be\n      75.3 - 65.4 = 9.9%, only ~64% more than Meta-Brain's 6.0%. This means routing quality\n      accounts for ~40% of the potential routing benefit left on the table. For the paper,\n      frame this as: \"PAR captures ~60% of the theoretically available routing benefit,\n      with the remaining gap attributable to soft-routing dilution and spatial ambiguity.\"\n      The gap also establishes headroom for future router improvements (e.g., confidence-gated\n      hard routing).\n\n  - name: \"Ablation Ladder — Frozen → Universal → Meta-Brain → Oracle\"\n    plan_id: plan_005\n    description: >\n      Systematic 5-seed comparison on full LIBERO-Plus: Frozen pi0, Universal LoRA (rank 16, ~2M),\n      Meta-Brain (PAR + 3 LoRA, ~6M), Oracle routing (ground-truth type). Report overall and\n      per-perturbation breakdown with statistical significance. Core evidence for Claim 2.\n    expected_results:\n      frozen_mean: 53.6\n      frozen_std: 1.8\n      universal_mean: 65.4\n      universal_std: 2.1\n      meta_brain_mean: 71.4\n      meta_brain_std: 2.4\n      oracle_mean: 75.3\n      oracle_std: 1.9\n      gain_frozen_to_universal: 11.8\n      gain_universal_to_meta_brain: 6.0\n      gain_meta_brain_to_oracle: 3.9\n      pvalue_universal_gt_frozen: 0.0003\n      pvalue_meta_brain_gt_universal: 0.008\n      pvalue_oracle_gt_meta_brain: 0.021\n      meta_brain_gain_over_frozen: 17.8\n    result_rationale: >\n      The ablation ladder shows a clean monotonic ordering (Frozen < Universal < Meta-Brain < Oracle)\n      with all adjacent pairs statistically significant at p < 0.05. Step sizes decrease going up\n      the ladder: +11.8 (baseline adaptation), +6.0 (routing benefit), +3.9 (routing quality gap).\n      The largest step is Frozen → Universal, confirming that simply adding LoRA adaptation\n      captures most of the low-hanging fruit. The Universal → Meta-Brain step (+6.0%) is the\n      headline number for Claim 2 — routing by perturbation type provides meaningful additional\n      benefit beyond uniform adaptation, but the advantage is moderate (not dominant). The\n      diminishing step sizes reflect a fundamental pattern: each successive refinement addresses\n      a smaller remaining error surface. Standard deviations are 1.8-2.4%, typical for LIBERO-Plus\n      with per-seed task ordering variance. Meta-Brain's higher std (2.4%) reflects routing\n      sensitivity to seed-specific perturbation distributions.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Ablation Ladder — Overall Success Rate (%, 5 seeds)\"\n        header: [Method, Params, Mean, Std, 95% CI, p vs. Previous]\n        rows:\n          - [Frozen pi0, 0, 53.6, 1.8, \"[51.4, 55.8]\", \"—\"]\n          - [Universal LoRA, ~2M, 65.4, 2.1, \"[62.8, 68.0]\", \"<0.001\"]\n          - [Meta-Brain, ~6M, 71.4, 2.4, \"[68.4, 74.4]\", \"0.008\"]\n          - [Oracle, ~6M, 75.3, 1.9, \"[72.9, 77.7]\", \"0.021\"]\n      - name: \"Ablation Ladder — Per-Perturbation Breakdown (%, 5-seed mean)\"\n        header: [Method, camera, light, bg, noise, distr, layout, robot, Overall]\n        rows:\n          - [Frozen, 47.6, 56.2, 51.8, 49.4, 58.6, 57.8, 53.8, 53.6]\n          - [Universal, 62.8, 69.4, 66.2, 63.0, 64.8, 62.6, 69.0, 65.4]\n          - [Meta-Brain, 71.0, 77.6, 74.4, 73.0, 65.2, 64.4, 74.2, 71.4]\n          - [Oracle, 75.2, 82.0, 78.4, 77.6, 69.4, 66.6, 78.0, 75.3]\n      - name: \"Routing Gain Decomposition by Coarse Type\"\n        header: [Coarse Type, Universal, Meta-Brain, Routing Gain, \"% of Oracle Ceiling\"]\n        rows:\n          - [Visual (4 types), 65.35, 74.0, +8.65, \"66.8%\"]\n          - [Spatial (2 types), 63.7, 64.8, +1.1, \"25.6%\"]\n          - [Physical (1 type), 69.0, 74.2, +5.2, \"57.8%\"]\n          - [Overall, 65.4, 71.4, +6.0, \"60.6%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Stacked bar chart (the ablation ladder): 4 methods on x-axis, success rate on y-axis.\n        Bars are colored in a gradient from red (Frozen) to green (Oracle). Error bars show ±1 std.\n        Horizontal dashed lines at 81.2% (VLA-GSE reference) and 53.6% (Frozen baseline). Clear\n        monotonic increase with decreasing step sizes. Significance brackets between adjacent pairs.\n      - >\n        Per-perturbation grouped bar chart: 7 perturbation types on x-axis, 4 methods as grouped\n        bars. Reveals the asymmetric routing benefit — large visual/physical separation between\n        Universal and Meta-Brain bars, minimal spatial separation. Oracle bars highest across all\n        types but layout/distractor gap is notably small.\n    analysis: >\n      The ablation ladder is clean and publishable. All pairwise comparisons are significant.\n      The key narrative point is that Meta-Brain captures 60.6% of the theoretically available\n      routing benefit (oracle ceiling). The per-type decomposition reveals that routing works\n      best where perturbations are localized (visual: 66.8% of ceiling captured) and least\n      where perturbations are distributed (spatial: 25.6%). This naturally leads to the\n      mechanistic analysis section in the paper explaining which processing stages each\n      perturbation type disrupts. For Claim 2, the headline is: \"Routing by perturbation\n      type improves over universal LoRA by 6.0 ± 2.3% (p = 0.008), with gains concentrated\n      on perturbation types that disrupt localized processing stages.\" This reframing\n      adds nuance rather than claiming universal superiority.\n\n  - name: \"FTM/FLA Reproduction — Visual-Only Baseline\"\n    plan_id: plan_006\n    description: >\n      Reproduce FTM (4K params) and FLA (4.7M params) on pi0 for visual perturbation types.\n      Compare with LoRA-V and Meta-Brain to establish visual-only baselines and demonstrate\n      Meta-Brain's multi-type advantage.\n    expected_results:\n      ftm_camera: 69.8\n      ftm_light: 76.4\n      ftm_background: 72.6\n      ftm_noise: 68.2\n      ftm_visual_avg: 71.75\n      ftm_overall: 65.3\n      fla_camera: 73.8\n      fla_light: 80.2\n      fla_background: 76.8\n      fla_noise: 75.4\n      fla_visual_avg: 76.55\n      fla_overall: 68.1\n      lora_v_visual_avg: 78.3\n      meta_brain_visual_avg: 74.0\n      meta_brain_overall: 71.4\n      meta_brain_advantage_over_ftm_overall: 6.1\n      meta_brain_advantage_over_fla_overall: 3.3\n    result_rationale: >\n      FTM at 4K params is remarkably parameter-efficient for visual perturbations (71.75% visual avg)\n      but fundamentally cannot handle spatial/physical types, leaving those at frozen baseline levels\n      and yielding only 65.3% overall. FLA at 4.7M params approaches LoRA-V quality on visual types\n      (76.55% vs 78.3%) but also cannot generalize beyond visual. Meta-Brain's overall advantage\n      (71.4% vs 65.3-68.1%) comes entirely from spatial and physical perturbation handling. On\n      visual types alone, LoRA-V (78.3%) > FLA (76.55%) > Meta-Brain routed (74.0%) > FTM (71.75%),\n      showing that specialized visual methods can match or exceed routing-based correction for\n      visual perturbations specifically. The key message: FTM/FLA are excellent visual-only\n      solutions but cannot serve as general robustness methods. Meta-Brain's value is breadth\n      of coverage, not per-type optimality. FTM's per-parameter efficiency on visual types\n      (11.7%/0.004M = 2925%/M) dwarfs all other methods but is limited to a single perturbation\n      modality.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Visual Perturbation Methods Comparison (%)\"\n        header: [Method, Params, camera, light, bg, noise, Visual Avg, Overall]\n        rows:\n          - [FTM, 4K, 69.8, 76.4, 72.6, 68.2, 71.75, 65.3]\n          - [FLA, 4.7M, 73.8, 80.2, 76.8, 75.4, 76.55, 68.1]\n          - [LoRA-V, ~2M, 75.2, 82.0, 78.4, 77.6, 78.3, 67.4]\n          - [Meta-Brain, ~6M, 71.0, 77.6, 74.4, 73.0, 74.0, 71.4]\n          - [Universal, ~2M, 62.8, 69.4, 66.2, 63.0, 65.35, 65.4]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Two-panel figure. Left: visual-types-only comparison (4 visual perturbation bars per\n        method). LoRA-V tops, FLA close second, Meta-Brain third, FTM fourth, Universal last.\n        Right: overall LIBERO-Plus comparison. Meta-Brain tops (71.4%), FLA second (68.1%),\n        LoRA-V/Universal/FTM clustered (65-67%). Shows the reversal: best visual-only method\n        is not the best overall method.\n    analysis: >\n      This experiment establishes two key points for the paper: (1) Visual-only methods are\n      competitive on visual perturbations but fail as general robustness solutions — there is\n      no free lunch. (2) Meta-Brain's routing approach provides the best overall coverage by\n      handling all perturbation types, even though it doesn't maximize any single type. For\n      Claim 3, FTM serves as an extreme point on the parameter-efficiency scatter plot: incredible\n      visual-only efficiency but zero spatial/physical coverage. Meta-Brain occupies a qualitatively\n      different position: moderate efficiency with universal coverage.\n\n  - name: \"Parameter Efficiency Analysis\"\n    plan_id: plan_007\n    description: >\n      Compute gain-over-frozen per method, normalize by trainable parameters. Produce the\n      parameter efficiency scatter plot (x=params log scale, y=gain-over-baseline). Include\n      Meta-Brain, Universal LoRA, FTM, FLA, VLA-GSE (from paper, different backbone caveat).\n    expected_results:\n      meta_brain_gain: 17.8\n      meta_brain_params_M: 6\n      meta_brain_gain_per_M: 2.97\n      universal_gain: 11.8\n      universal_params_M: 2\n      universal_gain_per_M: 5.9\n      ftm_gain: 11.7\n      ftm_params_M: 0.004\n      ftm_gain_per_M: 2925.0\n      fla_gain: 14.5\n      fla_params_M: 4.7\n      fla_gain_per_M: 3.09\n      vla_gse_gain: 27.6\n      vla_gse_params_M: 114\n      vla_gse_gain_per_M: 0.24\n      meta_brain_pct_of_vla_gse_gain: 64.5\n      meta_brain_pct_of_vla_gse_params: 5.3\n    result_rationale: >\n      Meta-Brain achieves 2.97%/M gain-per-parameter, sitting in the middle of the efficiency\n      spectrum. Universal LoRA is the most efficient multi-type method (5.9%/M) but has a lower\n      absolute gain ceiling. FTM is an extreme outlier (2925%/M) demonstrating that ultra-compact\n      methods exist for single-type perturbations, but this efficiency doesn't scale to multi-type\n      coverage. VLA-GSE achieves the highest absolute gain (27.6%) but at 0.24%/M efficiency —\n      a 12× parameter overhead vs Meta-Brain for 55% more gain. The headline for Claim 3:\n      \"Meta-Brain achieves 64.5% of VLA-GSE's robustness improvement using 5.3% of its trainable\n      parameters, demonstrating perturbation-diagnostic routing as a parameter-efficient alternative\n      to capacity-based expert routing.\" The different-backbone caveat (pi0 vs Qwen3-VL-4B) applies\n      to absolute numbers but the efficiency ratio is still informative.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Parameter Efficiency Comparison\"\n        header: [Method, Backbone, Params (M), Gain over Frozen (%), Gain/M Params, Coverage]\n        rows:\n          - [FTM, pi0, 0.004, 11.7, 2925.0, Visual only]\n          - [Universal LoRA, pi0, 2, 11.8, 5.9, All types]\n          - [FLA, pi0, 4.7, 14.5, 3.09, Visual only]\n          - [Meta-Brain, pi0, 6, 17.8, 2.97, All types]\n          - [VLA-GSE†, Qwen3-VL-4B, 114, 27.6, 0.24, All types]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Scatter plot: x-axis = trainable parameters (log scale, 0.001M to 200M), y-axis = gain\n        over frozen baseline (%). Each method is a labeled point with marker size proportional to\n        coverage (all-type methods larger). FTM is far left (tiny params, moderate gain). Universal\n        LoRA and Meta-Brain are center-left. VLA-GSE is far right (high gain, huge params). A\n        Pareto frontier line connects FTM → Universal → Meta-Brain, showing that Meta-Brain extends\n        the efficiency frontier beyond universal adaptation. VLA-GSE sits above the frontier but\n        at 19× the cost. Dashed diagonal lines show iso-efficiency contours (%/M). Annotation:\n        \"Meta-Brain: 65% of VLA-GSE gain at 5% parameters.\" VLA-GSE point marked with † for\n        different backbone.\n    analysis: >\n      The scatter plot is the visual centerpiece of Claim 3. The key insight is that Meta-Brain\n      extends the Pareto frontier established by Universal LoRA — it proves that routing-based\n      specialization extracts more gain per parameter than simply increasing uniform LoRA rank.\n      The framing should be \"complementary to capacity routing, not replacement\": Meta-Brain\n      operates in a different region of the parameter-efficiency space than VLA-GSE. For\n      resource-constrained deployment (edge devices, real-time control), Meta-Brain offers\n      a practical operating point. An ideal system might compose both: Meta-Brain for\n      type-aware correction + capacity routing for residual error.\n\n  - name: \"Correction Memory Ablation — With/Without CM\"\n    plan_id: plan_008\n    description: >\n      Compare Meta-Brain with and without Correction Memory module. CM modulates routing\n      weights using retrieved exemplars from a FAISS-indexed memory bank. Quantify marginal\n      gain overall and per perturbation type.\n    expected_results:\n      meta_brain_no_cm: 71.4\n      meta_brain_with_cm: 72.6\n      cm_ablation_delta: 1.2\n      cm_delta_visual: 0.8\n      cm_delta_spatial: 0.4\n      cm_delta_physical: 2.8\n      cm_alpha_learned: 0.23\n      cm_retrieval_latency_ms: 1.4\n    result_rationale: >\n      CM provides a marginal +1.2% overall gain, concentrated on physical perturbations (+2.8%)\n      where the same robot dynamics changes recur consistently across episodes — exemplar\n      retrieval identifies \"I've seen this perturbation before\" and sharpens the routing weight\n      toward LoRA-P. Visual gain is small (+0.8%) because the PAR router already routes visual\n      perturbations confidently (diag 0.82) and CM's modulation has little room to improve.\n      Spatial gain is minimal (+0.4%) because spatial perturbations are fundamentally different\n      each time (different object positions) so retrieved exemplars provide weak signal. The\n      learned alpha parameter (0.23) confirms the system's own assessment: CM contributes\n      ~23% weighting to the final routing decision, indicating it's a secondary correction to\n      PAR, not a primary signal. The 1.4ms retrieval latency adds negligible overhead to the\n      10Hz control loop. CM is worth including as an optional module but does not change\n      the core story.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Correction Memory Ablation (%)\"\n        header: [Configuration, Visual, Spatial, Physical, Overall]\n        rows:\n          - [Meta-Brain (no CM), 74.0, 64.8, 74.2, 71.4]\n          - [Meta-Brain + CM, 74.8, 65.2, 77.0, 72.6]\n          - [CM Delta, +0.8, +0.4, +2.8, +1.2]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Grouped bar chart: 3 coarse types + overall on x-axis, paired bars (with/without CM).\n        Physical bar shows visible CM benefit. Visual and spatial bars nearly identical.\n        Error bars from 3 seeds. The difference is within 1 std for visual/spatial but\n        marginally significant for physical.\n    analysis: >\n      CM's marginal contribution (1.2%) is expected in the median tier. It validates the module\n      as a sound idea (positive delta on all types) but not a paper-defining contribution.\n      Present CM as \"optional module, +1.2% overall, primarily benefiting recurrent physical\n      perturbations.\" The per-type pattern adds a small mechanistic insight: exemplar-based\n      routing modulation works best when perturbations are consistent (physics changes) rather\n      than combinatorially varied (spatial layouts). For the paper, CM goes in the ablation\n      section with 1-2 sentences, not a dedicated section.\n\n  - name: \"Router Architecture Comparison — MLP vs Linear vs Attention\"\n    plan_id: plan_009\n    description: >\n      Compare PAR router variants on PaliGemma layer 6: (a) 2-layer MLP (default, ~50K params),\n      (b) linear classifier (~6K params), (c) cross-attention router (~120K params). Evaluate\n      both routing accuracy and downstream task success to test whether classification accuracy\n      improvements translate to task performance.\n    expected_results:\n      mlp_routing_accuracy: 93.8\n      mlp_task_success: 71.4\n      mlp_params: 50000\n      linear_routing_accuracy: 90.6\n      linear_task_success: 70.2\n      linear_params: 6000\n      attention_routing_accuracy: 94.2\n      attention_task_success: 71.2\n      attention_params: 120000\n      mlp_vs_linear_task_gap: 1.2\n      mlp_vs_attention_task_gap: 0.2\n    result_rationale: >\n      MLP (default) achieves 93.8% routing accuracy and 71.4% task success, offering the best\n      cost/performance tradeoff. Linear router loses 3.2% routing accuracy, which translates to\n      a modest 1.2% task success drop — the per-token representation at layer 6 is already\n      quite separable (Phase 0 showed F1=0.993 with MLP), so a linear boundary captures most\n      of the structure but misses some nonlinear decision boundaries near cluster edges.\n      Attention router adds 0.4% routing accuracy over MLP (the classes are well-separated\n      enough that attention over token sequences adds minimal information beyond mean pooling)\n      but this doesn't translate to task success improvement (71.2% vs 71.4%) — the 0.4%\n      classification gain falls within the noise floor of rollout evaluation. The attention\n      router's 2.4× parameter overhead is unjustified. Conclusion: MLP is the right choice,\n      confirming the Phase 0 finding that perturbation type is a simple classification problem\n      at layer 6.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Router Architecture Comparison\"\n        header: [Router, Params, Routing Acc (%), Task Success (%), Inference Overhead (ms)]\n        rows:\n          - [Linear, 6K, 90.6, 70.2, 0.02]\n          - [MLP (default), 50K, 93.8, 71.4, 0.05]\n          - [Cross-Attention, 120K, 94.2, 71.2, 0.18]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Dual-axis scatter: x = routing classification accuracy (%), left y = task success (%),\n        right y = router parameter count. Three points (Linear, MLP, Attention). Shows that\n        routing accuracy improvements beyond ~93% yield diminishing task success returns. The\n        gap between routing accuracy and task success is largest for Attention, illustrating\n        the disconnect between classification metrics and control metrics.\n    analysis: >\n      This experiment addresses reviewer concern C1 (\"Phase 0 classification is too easy\") from\n      a different angle: yes, the classification is relatively easy (even a linear classifier gets\n      90.6%), but the task-success gap between 90.6% and 93.8% routing accuracy is 1.2% —\n      confirming that routing accuracy does matter at the margins. The MLP's 3.2% classification\n      advantage over linear translates to 1.2% task success gain, roughly 1:0.37 ratio. Beyond\n      93.8%, additional classification gains have near-zero marginal task benefit. This helps\n      calibrate the oracle-PAR gap analysis: the 3.9% task gap from oracle routing quality\n      corresponds to moving from ~93.8% to ~100% classification accuracy, and the nonlinear\n      mapping suggests diminishing returns as routing approaches perfection.\n\n  - name: \"6-Class vs 3-Class Routing Comparison\"\n    plan_id: plan_010\n    description: >\n      Compare fine-grained 6-class routing (camera/light/background/noise/layout/robot, 6 LoRA\n      experts ~12M params) with coarse 3-class routing (Visual/Spatial/Physical, 3 experts ~6M).\n      Tests whether finer-grained perturbation-type routing justifies additional parameters and\n      architectural complexity.\n    expected_results:\n      three_class_overall: 71.4\n      three_class_routing_accuracy: 93.8\n      three_class_params_M: 6\n      six_class_overall: 72.2\n      six_class_routing_accuracy: 89.4\n      six_class_params_M: 12\n      delta_overall: 0.8\n      six_class_camera: 72.8\n      six_class_light: 79.2\n      six_class_background: 76.0\n      six_class_noise: 74.6\n      six_class_distractor: 66.0\n      six_class_layout: 65.2\n      six_class_robot: 81.6\n    result_rationale: >\n      6-class routing provides only +0.8% overall improvement over 3-class while doubling the\n      parameter budget (12M vs 6M) and dropping routing accuracy by 4.4% (89.4% vs 93.8%).\n      The marginal gain arises primarily from the robot expert (+3.8% from dedicated robot LoRA\n      vs shared Physical LoRA) where the 1-type Physical class benefits from fully dedicated\n      parameters. Intra-Visual perturbation types (camera/light/background/noise) do NOT benefit\n      significantly from separate experts because they share the same failure mode (early\n      representation corruption) — Phase 0 6-class analysis already showed high intra-Visual\n      similarity. The spatial split (distractor/layout) gains marginally because both types\n      require trajectory-level reasoning that LoRA cannot provide. The 4.4% routing accuracy\n      drop with 6 classes partially offsets any per-expert specialization gain: more classes\n      means more routing errors, and each error routes to a more narrowly specialized (thus\n      more harmful when mismatched) expert. Net assessment: 3-class is the better design point.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"6-Class vs 3-Class Comparison (%)\"\n        header: [Configuration, Classes, Params, Routing Acc, Overall, Δ vs 3-class]\n        rows:\n          - [3-class Meta-Brain, 3, ~6M, 93.8, 71.4, \"—\"]\n          - [6-class Meta-Brain, 6, ~12M, 89.4, 72.2, +0.8]\n      - name: \"6-Class Per-Type Breakdown vs 3-Class (%)\"\n        header: [Perturbation, 3-class, 6-class, Delta]\n        rows:\n          - [camera, 71.0, 72.8, +1.8]\n          - [light, 77.6, 79.2, +1.6]\n          - [background, 74.4, 76.0, +1.6]\n          - [noise, 73.0, 74.6, +1.6]\n          - [distractor, 65.2, 66.0, +0.8]\n          - [layout, 64.4, 65.2, +0.8]\n          - [robot, 74.2, 81.6, +7.4]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Stacked cost-benefit bar: left bar = 3-class (6M params, 71.4% success), right bar =\n        6-class (12M params, 72.2% success). The 2× parameter cost is visually dominant for a\n        barely visible success rate gain. Annotation: \"+0.8% for 2× params = poor ROI.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This directly addresses reviewer concern C5 (\"3-class taxonomy may be too coarse\"). The\n      answer is: 3-class is nearly optimal for this architecture. The 6-class robot improvement\n      (+7.4%) is notable in isolation but doesn't offset the systemic costs of finer routing.\n      The Phase 0 intra-Visual similarity finding (6-class accuracy 99.82% but intra-Visual\n      confusion is near-zero) predicted this outcome: types that cluster together in activation\n      space don't need separate experts. Include this as a 1-paragraph ablation result: \"Finer\n      6-class routing adds +0.8% at 2× parameters, confirming that the 3-class taxonomy aligns\n      with the mechanistic distinction in VLA processing stages.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cross-Backbone Validation — SmolVLA\"\n    plan_id: plan_011\n    description: >\n      Replicate Phase 0 classification + Phase 1 routing pipeline on SmolVLA to test whether\n      perturbation-diagnostic routing generalizes beyond pi0. SmolVLA uses a different vision\n      encoder (SigLIP) and language backbone (SmolLM), testing architecture independence.\n    expected_results:\n      smolvla_phase0_3class_f1: 0.938\n      smolvla_phase0_best_layer: 4\n      smolvla_frozen_baseline: 46.2\n      smolvla_universal_lora: 56.8\n      smolvla_meta_brain: 62.4\n      smolvla_gain_over_frozen: 16.2\n      smolvla_gain_over_universal: 5.6\n      smolvla_routing_diagonality: 0.73\n      smolvla_visual_specialization_delta: 10.4\n      smolvla_spatial_specialization_delta: 2.8\n      smolvla_physical_specialization_delta: 7.6\n    result_rationale: >\n      SmolVLA confirms the core finding: frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type\n      (F1=0.938, lower than pi0's 0.993 due to SigLIP's different feature structure but still\n      high). The best classification layer shifts to layer 4 (SmolVLA has fewer layers than\n      PaliGemma). Meta-Brain on SmolVLA achieves +16.2% gain over frozen (comparable to pi0's\n      +17.8%) and +5.6% over universal (comparable to pi0's +6.0%), demonstrating that the\n      routing benefit is architecture-independent. Absolute numbers are lower (62.4% vs 71.4%)\n      due to SmolVLA's weaker backbone capacity (SmolLM-360M vs pi0's larger model). The\n      per-type specialization pattern replicates: visual shows the largest delta (10.4%),\n      physical moderate (7.6%), spatial weak (2.8%). Spatial underperformance persists across\n      backbones, confirming it's a fundamental limitation of single-step LoRA correction for\n      trajectory-level perturbations, not an artifact of pi0's architecture. Routing diagonality\n      (0.73) is slightly lower than pi0 (0.77), reflecting SigLIP's less structured intermediate\n      representations for perturbation classification.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Cross-Backbone Comparison (pi0 vs SmolVLA)\"\n        header: [Metric, pi0, SmolVLA]\n        rows:\n          - [Phase 0 3-class F1, 0.993, 0.938]\n          - [Best classification layer, 6, 4]\n          - [Frozen baseline (%), 53.6, 46.2]\n          - [Universal LoRA (%), 65.4, 56.8]\n          - [Meta-Brain (%), 71.4, 62.4]\n          - [Gain over frozen (%), 17.8, 16.2]\n          - [Gain over universal (%), 6.0, 5.6]\n          - [Routing diagonality, 0.77, 0.73]\n          - [Visual specialization delta (%), 12.95, 10.4]\n          - [Spatial specialization delta (%), 4.3, 2.8]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Side-by-side ablation ladders for pi0 and SmolVLA. Both show the same monotonic\n        ordering (Frozen < Universal < Meta-Brain) with similar step sizes. Pi0 bars are\n        uniformly higher (stronger backbone) but the relative gains are comparable. Annotation\n        highlights the consistent routing benefit across architectures.\n    analysis: >\n      This experiment addresses reviewer concern C3 (\"single backbone, limited generalizability\").\n      The result is clearly positive: the qualitative pattern replicates exactly (same per-type\n      specialization ranking, same ablation ordering, comparable relative gains). The Phase 0\n      F1 drop (0.993 → 0.938) is expected — SigLIP and PaliGemma have different feature\n      structures — but 0.938 is still high enough for effective routing. The spatial\n      underperformance replication across backbones strengthens the mechanistic interpretation:\n      it's a property of the perturbation type, not the backbone. For the paper, include as\n      a validation subsection: \"Cross-backbone results on SmolVLA replicate the qualitative\n      findings, with comparable routing gains (+5.6% vs +6.0% over universal), confirming\n      that perturbation-diagnostic routing is an architecture-independent principle.\"\n      This is a P2 experiment — include if time/GPU budget permits, otherwise cite the\n      Phase 0 cross-backbone evidence from SAFE/probing literature.\n\n  - name: \"LoRA Layer Placement Ablation\"\n    plan_id: plan_012\n    description: >\n      Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA: (a) early (2-6), (b) mid (6-12), (c) late\n      (12-16), (d) all layers. Test hypothesis that perturbation types optimally correct at\n      different processing stages.\n    expected_results:\n      lora_v_early: 80.2\n      lora_v_mid: 74.8\n      lora_v_late: 71.4\n      lora_v_all: 78.3\n      lora_s_early: 64.6\n      lora_s_mid: 70.2\n      lora_s_late: 66.8\n      lora_s_all: 68.0\n      lora_p_early: 72.8\n      lora_p_mid: 75.2\n      lora_p_late: 80.6\n      lora_p_all: 78.0\n      optimal_placement_v: \"early (2-6)\"\n      optimal_placement_s: \"mid (6-12)\"\n      optimal_placement_p: \"late (12-16)\"\n      optimal_combined_overall: 73.8\n      all_layer_combined_overall: 71.4\n      layer_placement_gain: 2.4\n    result_rationale: >\n      The layer placement results support the mechanistic hypothesis with moderate effect sizes.\n      LoRA-V benefits most from early layers (+1.9% over all-layer) because visual perturbations\n      (camera/light/background/noise) corrupt the initial visual encoding in PaliGemma's early\n      layers — correcting at the source prevents error propagation. LoRA-S benefits from mid\n      layers (+2.2% over all-layer) because spatial reasoning (object relationships, layout\n      understanding) is computed in middle transformer layers where multi-head attention\n      integrates spatial context — though the absolute improvement remains modest due to the\n      fundamental trajectory-level limitation. LoRA-P benefits from late layers (+2.6% over\n      all-layer) because physical perturbations manifest at the action prediction stage in\n      late layers where dynamics models are implicitly computed. All-layer placement performs\n      as a reasonable default (second-best for each expert type) because the LoRA rank is\n      distributed across layers that contribute varying amounts. The combined optimal placement\n      (type-specific layers per expert) yields 73.8% overall (+2.4% over uniform all-layer\n      71.4%), suggesting layer-aware routing is a promising extension.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Layer Placement × Expert Type (own-type success rate %)\"\n        header: [Layer Range, LoRA-V (Visual), LoRA-S (Spatial), LoRA-P (Physical)]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Early (2-6)\", 80.2, 64.6, 72.8]\n          - [\"Mid (6-12)\", 74.8, 70.2, 75.2]\n          - [\"Late (12-16)\", 71.4, 66.8, 80.6]\n          - [\"All layers\", 78.3, 68.0, 78.0]\n      - name: \"Optimal Layer Placement Summary\"\n        header: [Expert, Best Layers, Own-Type Success, Δ vs All-Layer]\n        rows:\n          - [LoRA-V, \"Early (2-6)\", 80.2, +1.9]\n          - [LoRA-S, \"Mid (6-12)\", 70.2, +2.2]\n          - [LoRA-P, \"Late (12-16)\", 80.6, +2.6]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        3-panel line chart: one panel per expert type. X-axis = layer range (early/mid/late/all),\n        y-axis = own-type success rate. Each panel shows a different peak: LoRA-V peaks early,\n        LoRA-S peaks mid, LoRA-P peaks late. All-layer performance marked with a dashed horizontal\n        line in each panel for reference. The crossed peak pattern visually demonstrates that\n        different perturbation types optimally correct at different processing stages.\n    analysis: >\n      This is a P2 experiment that adds mechanistic depth but is not essential for the core\n      claims. The crossed peak pattern (V-early, S-mid, P-late) aligns with how VLA processing\n      stages work: visual encoding → spatial reasoning → action prediction. This provides\n      evidence for the paper's framing that perturbation types disrupt different processing\n      stages. The +2.4% gain from optimal layer placement is small but suggests a natural\n      extension: layer-aware routing that not only selects the right expert but applies it\n      at the right layers. This could close part of the oracle-PAR gap (3.9%) without\n      improving classification accuracy. For the paper, include as a supplementary analysis\n      with the crossed-peak figure — it's visually compelling and mechanistically interpretive.\n      Do not pursue layer-specific routing as a primary contribution; it's a natural future\n      work direction.\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"b5927951-3a29-4d8c-8713-a1fb502a4f6d","total_cost_usd":1.1160277500000002,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cach